---
import PlaygroundShell from '../../components/PlaygroundShell.astro';
---
<PlaygroundShell title="Two devices edit the same record offline" hint="Two devices change one record while offline, then both sync. How the server merges the changes decides which edits survive. 600 simulated pairs of devices, about 1,900 edits, the same every time.">
  <ol class="how">
    <li>Keep the default, <b>Per field, newest device clock wins</b> and a clock error of 5 minutes: <b>139 of 1,897 edits are lost</b>. 84 of those are a stock count changed on two devices at once, where one change overwrites the other. The other 55 are caused only by the clock error: a device that is 5 minutes fast wins against a person who edited later, after seeing its change.</li>
    <li>Switch to <b>Per field, hybrid logical clock</b>: the 55 disappear, because an edit made after seeing another change always gets a later timestamp, whatever the wall clock says. 84 are still lost, because last-writer-wins cannot add two changes to a count.</li>
    <li>Switch to <b>Per field, hybrid clock, counters add up, text conflicts ask</b>: <b>0 edits lost</b>. Counts are added together, and the <b>200</b> real same-field conflicts on text fields are shown to the user instead of settled by a clock. Whole-record last-writer-wins is worst: <b>602</b> lost at 5 minutes, because the loser's other fields go too.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="pg osb" data-osb>
    <div class="pg-row">
      <label>How the server merges <select class="pg-in" data-pol>
        <option value="0">Whole record, newest device clock wins</option>
        <option value="1" selected>Per field, newest device clock wins</option>
        <option value="2">Per field, hybrid logical clock</option>
        <option value="3">Per field, hybrid clock, counters add up, text conflicts asked</option>
      </select></label>
      <label>Clock error on one of the two devices <select class="pg-in" data-skew>
        <option value="0">None</option>
        <option value="60">1 minute</option>
        <option value="300" selected>5 minutes</option>
        <option value="3600">1 hour</option>
      </select></label>
    </div>
    <div class="pg-grid">
      <div class="pg-box" data-b1><h3>Edits lost</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o1></span><span class="pg-note" data-n1></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b2><h3>Lost only because of the clock error</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o2></span><span class="pg-note" data-n2></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b3><h3>Same-field conflicts on text</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o3></span><span class="pg-note" data-n3></span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="pg-out" data-verdict aria-live="polite"></p>
    <p class="pg-note">Model: each of 600 pairs edits a record with a title, a status and a stock count. Each device changes each field with probability one half. In 40% of pairs the second device had already pulled the first one's change (so its edit is deliberate and should win), and in 60% both edited blind. An edit is lost if it should have survived and is not in the final record: an informed edit that is overwritten, an edit to a field nobody else touched, or a stock-count change made on both devices at once. The clock error is added or subtracted at random to one device of each pair.</p>
  </div>
</PlaygroundShell>
<script>
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-osb]').forEach((root) => {
    const q = <T extends HTMLElement>(s: string) => root.querySelector<T>(s)!;
    const PAIRS = 600; const FIELDS = ['title', 'status', 'qty'];
    const POLICY = { record: 0, field: 1, hlc: 2, merge: 3 }; // values of the policy select
    const SEED = 4242;
    const CAUSAL_RATE = 0.4; // share of pairs where the second edit was made after seeing the first
    const MAX_GAP_S = 600; // real time between the two edits: 1..600 s
    function sim(policy: number, skew: number) {
      // linear congruential generator (Numerical Recipes constants)
      let seed = SEED; const rnd = () => ((seed = (seed * 1664525 + 1013904223) % 4294967296) / 4294967296);
      let edits = 0; let lost = 0; let conflicts = 0;
      for (let i = 0; i < PAIRS; i++) {
        const causal = rnd() < CAUSAL_RATE; const skewed = rnd() < 0.5 ? 0 : 1; const sign = rnd() < 0.5 ? 1 : -1; const gap = 1 + Math.floor(rnd() * MAX_GAP_S);
        const e: Set<string>[] = [new Set(), new Set()];
        for (const d of [0, 1]) { FIELDS.forEach((f) => { if (rnd() < 0.5) e[d].add(f); }); if (!e[d].size) e[d].add(FIELDS[Math.floor(rnd() * FIELDS.length)]); }
        const ts = [skewed === 0 ? sign * skew : 0, gap + (skewed === 1 ? sign * skew : 0)];
        if (policy >= POLICY.hlc && causal) ts[1] = Math.max(ts[1], ts[0] + 1); // a hybrid clock never goes behind a change this device has seen
        const winner = ts[1] >= ts[0] ? 1 : 0;
        for (const d of [0, 1]) for (const f of e[d]) {
          edits++;
          const other = e[1 - d].has(f);
          const should = !other || (causal ? d === 1 : f === 'qty');
          let survives: boolean;
          if (policy === POLICY.record) survives = d === winner;
          else if (!other) survives = true;
          else if (policy === POLICY.merge && f === 'qty') survives = true; // counts are added, so both changes count
          else survives = d === winner;
          if (should && !survives) lost++;
        }
        if (!causal) for (const f of ['title', 'status']) if (e[0].has(f) && e[1].has(f)) conflicts++;
      }
      return { edits, lost, conflicts };
    }
    const fmt = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-US');
    function draw() {
      const policy = Number(q<HTMLSelectElement>('[data-pol]').value); const skew = Number(q<HTMLSelectElement>('[data-skew]').value);
      const r = sim(policy, skew); const base = sim(policy, 0); const clockLoss = r.lost - base.lost;
      q('[data-o1]').textContent = `${fmt(r.lost)} of ${fmt(r.edits)}`; q('[data-n1]').textContent = `${(100 * r.lost / r.edits).toFixed(1)}% of all edits`;
      q('[data-o2]').textContent = fmt(clockLoss); q('[data-n2]').textContent = skew === 0 ? 'no clock error in this run' : `${fmt(base.lost)} would be lost even with perfect clocks`;
      q('[data-o3]').textContent = fmt(r.conflicts); q('[data-n3]').textContent = policy === POLICY.merge ? 'shown to the user to choose' : 'settled silently by the clock order';
      q<HTMLElement>('[data-b1]').style.setProperty('--c', r.lost === 0 ? 'var(--good)' : 'var(--bad)');
      q<HTMLElement>('[data-b2]').style.setProperty('--c', clockLoss === 0 ? 'var(--good)' : 'var(--bad)');
      q<HTMLElement>('[data-b3]').style.setProperty('--c', policy === POLICY.merge ? 'var(--good)' : 'var(--accent)');
      const out = q('[data-verdict]'); out.classList.toggle('bad', r.lost > 0);
      out.textContent = r.lost === 0 ? `No edit is lost. Counts add up and the ${fmt(r.conflicts)} real conflicts on text fields go to the user, because no clock can say which of two blind edits was meant to win.`
        : policy === POLICY.record ? `${fmt(r.lost)} edits are lost: the loser of a whole-record comparison takes every field it changed with it, even fields the winner never touched.`
        : clockLoss > 0 ? `${fmt(r.lost)} edits are lost, ${fmt(clockLoss)} of them only because a device clock was wrong. A later edit made after seeing the other change lost to a clock that runs fast.`
        : `${fmt(r.lost)} edits are lost, all of them stock-count changes made on both devices at once: last-writer-wins keeps one number and drops the other change.`;
    }
    root.addEventListener('input', draw); root.addEventListener('change', draw);
    draw();
  });
</script>
